\section{Where R finds \verb{:=}}{

\verb{:=} is datakit's, re-exported: it takes the left side apart and
calls datakit's \code{colon_equals()}, whose method for dibbles is
dtatools'. On any other data frame it follows R's rules (see
\code{?datakit::":="}). rlang and data.table each export a \verb{:=} too, only
to report misuse, and the package attached last is the one R finds.
Attach dtatools after them, or, in tests that call \verb{dtatools::}
functions without attaching it, bind it in a helper file:
\code{`:=` <- dtatools::`:=`}. Code in a package that imports rlang's
\verb{:=} must bind dtatools' the same way. Their own uses of \verb{:=}, such
as data.table's \code{dt[, b := 1]} or dplyr's \code{mutate(d, !!nm := 1)},
keep working, as they read \verb{:=} without calling it.
}

\section{Assigning with \verb{<-}}{

\code{d[i, name] <- value} also changes \code{d}, and needs no attached
package, as R finds \verb{[<-} by the dibble's class. It does what
\code{d[i, name] := value} does, with \code{i} evaluated among the columns
as there, except for the following:
\itemize{
\item R evaluates \code{value} before the dibble sees it, in the caller's
environment, so it cannot use the columns: \code{d[x == 1, y] <- 0} works,
but for \code{d[x == 1, y] <- z * 2}, write \code{d[x == 1, y] := z * 2}.
\code{value} has one element, or one per row \code{i} picks (not one per row
of \code{d}). It is evaluated even when \code{i} picks no rows.
\item There is no \code{by}.
\item \code{name} is a column name, a string (\code{"a"}), or a variable in
parentheses (\code{(nm)}). A bare name that is not a column but is a
variable in the caller, as in \code{for (nm in vars) d[x > 0, nm] <- 1}
or a helper's \code{d[i, col] <- v}, is an error, since tibble would read
it as the variable and \verb{:=} as the column; write \code{(nm)} or \code{"nm"}.
\item It cannot add rows: write \code{d[nrow(d) + 1, ] <- list(...)}.
\item \code{d[, name] <- NULL} drops the column; with \code{i} it is an error.
}

Any other \verb{[<-} (\code{d[i] <- v}, a numeric \code{j}, or several columns) is
tibble's, with \code{i} evaluated in the caller.
